From f0348566c76848956264decb2fccca12a068485e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reixo <125610085+Reixo0@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:11:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 307/307] PCI: qcom: hold the Wi-Fi link through system suspend
 with CESTA

On Kaanapali the PCIe0 link to the Wi-Fi chip can be handed to CESTA,
the hardware that keeps it in L1ss while the system sleeps and wakes it
for the endpoint, as Android does. Enable that for system suspend with
wake-on-WLAN armed.

- dts: a CRM node for PCIe (pcie_crm) and, on pcie0, the CRM link, the
  PCIe state machine and PDC regions, and the vendor state machine
  program.
- pcie-qcom: load the state machine and vote D0 through the CRM HW DRV
  at bring-up. In suspend_noirq, with an endpoint armed for wake, set
  L1SS_SLEEP, route CLKREQ# through the PDC, vote DRV, then let go of
  every PCIe clock: from there the CRM sequencer owns the clock tree
  (core GDSC, pipe mux, rchng clock, TCSR refclk, rails) and stalls if
  Linux still holds any of it. Resume takes the clocks back, wakes the
  link and votes D0.
- phy-qcom-qmp-pcie: PHY_PCIE_CESTA_ENTER/EXIT drop and restore all PHY
  clocks, the refclk included, and keep the PHY GDSC on the wakeup path.
- pinctrl-msm: msm_gpio_wakeup_set() for the CLKREQ# wake.
- crm: HW DRV PERF_OL and power state writes, BW_PT zero votes.
- gcc-kaanapali: gcc_pcie_0_gdsc is PWRSTS_OFF_ON (as sm8750), so genpd
  really drops Linux's vote in suspend; with RET_ON the sequencer could
  not collapse the GDSC and the D0 vote at resume never completed.

Without CONFIG_QCOM_CRM the link takes the plain suspend path as before.

Tested on the RedMagic 11 Pro at Gen2 and Gen3: pm_test=platform, and a
real s2idle of 108 s woken by a Wi-Fi magic packet; the normal suspend
path (wake-on-WLAN off) still resumes with the GDSC really off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
